n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></th></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $P(X = 1, Y = 1) = P(X = 1) \\cdot P(Y = 1)$\n2. $P(X = x, Y = y) = P(X = x) \\cdot P(Y = y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n3. $X$ and $Y$ are independent\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186162472 :question/problem "An email server manager determined that $4 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of the emails their clients receive are spam. After introducing a new filtering feature, the manager wants to know if the proportion of spam emails their clients receive has decreased. They decide to conduct a hypothesis test at the $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level.\n\nThe manager samples $100$ client emails and notices that only $1$ is spam. The manager compares this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the following cumulative distribution table for $X∼B(100, 0.04)$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.0169$ | $0.0872$ | $0.2321$ | $0.4295$ | $0.6289$ |\n\nFill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of spam emails in a random sample of $100$, then a suitable test statistic is\n$X = 1$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nSince $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $10 \\mathrm{\\% } ,$ there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.\n\nTherefore, we {{field-6}} that the proportion of spam emails has reduced since the filtering feature was installed." "An email server manager determined that $4 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of the emails their clients receive are spam. After introducing a new filtering feature, the manager wants to know if the proportion of spam emails their clients receive has decreased. They decide to conduct a hypothesis test at the $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level.\n\nThe manager samples $100$ client emails