data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\quad p \\quad$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.70$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.80$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.85$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.90$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.95$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Phi^{-1}(p)$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.3853\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.5244\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.6745\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.8416\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.0364\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.2816\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.6449\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186166711 :question/worked-solution "![](images/fb/fb7c6c77767368871d3b39991093906f6a4a0f94e2a83075570e22b3f0e87a78.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-{y}^{2}(y+4) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y <                                    -4$ | $-4 <                                    y <                                    0$ | $y > 0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny' = -y^2(y+4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y' = -y^2(y+4).$" "![](images/fb/fb7c6c77767368871d3b39991093906f6a4a0f94e2a83075570e22b3f0e87a78.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-{y}^{2}(y+4) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Region</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$y <                                    -4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$-4 <                                    y <                                    0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$y > 0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Slope</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sign</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny' = -y^2(y+4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y' = -y^2(y+4).$"] [:db/cas 17592186166713 :question/worked-solution "![](images/b3/b3768fbcd892e8484f0fabfca29bd6365cfc382bde2dd39c6ca8d278911747db.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x = -2$ and $x = 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x <                  -2$ | $-2 <                  x <                  2$ | $x > 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| Slope | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny' =x^2 - 4 = (x+2)(x-2)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny' = x^2 - 4x + 4 = (x-2)^2\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=2,$ but not at $x=-2.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny' = 4 - x^2 = (2+x)(2-x)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) does not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x <                  -2$ | $-2 <                  x <                  2$ | $x > 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "![](images/b3/b3768fbcd892e8484f0fabfca29bd6365cfc382bde2dd39c6ca8d278911747db.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x = -2$ and $x = 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Region</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x <                  -2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$-2 <                  x <                  2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x > 2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Slope</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Slope</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny' =x^2 - 4 = (x+2)(x-2)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny' = x^2 - 4x + 4 = (x-2)^2\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=2,$ but not at $x=-2.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny' = 4 - x^2 = (2+x)(2-x)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) does not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Region</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x <                  -2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$-2 <                  x <                  2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x > 2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Slope</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sign</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186166714 :question/worked-solution "![](images/96/96fa01db29c0effec6c81996cfcaad1d46ffa71f2f1b2b3da0a48dc39bfb0d26.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\ny'= y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y = y(y^2 - 4y + 4) = y(y-2)^2\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\ny'=  y^2 - 2y = y(y-2).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y <        0$ | $0 <          y <          2$ | $y > 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny ' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y =y(y-2)^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y' =y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y.$" "![](images/96/96fa01db29c0effec6c81996cfcaad1d46ffa71f2f1b2b3da0a48dc39bfb0d26.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\ny'= y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y = y(y^2 - 4y + 4) = y(y-2)^2\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\ny'=  y^2 - 2y = y(y-2).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Region</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$y <        0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$0 <          y <          2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$y > 2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Slope</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sign</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny ' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y =y(y-2)^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y' =y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y.$"] [:db/cas 17592186167142 :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the binomial random variable $X∼B(6, 0.54)$ given below, compute $P(X < 4)$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.0095$ | $0.0762$ | $0.2721$ | $0.5786$ | $0.8485$ | $0.9752$ | $1.0000$ |" "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the binomial random variable $X∼B(6, 0.54)$ given below, compute $P(X < 4)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X \\le x)$</td><td>$0.0095$</td>\n<td>$0.0762$</td>\n<td>$0.2721$</td>\n<td>$0.5786$</td>\n<td>$0.8485$</td>\n<td>$0.9752$</td>\n<td>$1.0000$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186167143 :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the binomial random variable $X∼B(6, 0.41)$ given below, compute $P(2 < X \\le 5)$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.0422$ | $0.2181$ | $0.5236$ | $0.8067$ | $0.9542$ | $0.9952$ | $1.0000$ |" "Using the cumulative distribution function table of th